{
  "image_name": "22dfc988e13e696e86bf1c03e09155ef.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure outlines a workflow for identifying and testing beneficial coral-associated microbes. It begins with the coral microbiome and splits into two complementary strategies. One uses meta-omics comparisons of healthy versus bleached corals, followed by bioinformatics and statistical analysis to find a “core” set of taxa or pathways and predict a minimal microbiome. The other starts from culturing individual microbes, then analyzing their genomes, plasmids, and phenotypes to evaluate useful traits.\n\nThese paths converge in the construction of synthetic microbiomes that are inoculated into coral hosts and tested, apparently for effects on bleaching or thermal stress, as suggested by the coral icons and thermometers. A second branch emphasizes highly specific symbionts, especially maternally transmitted or obligate associates. These are isolated in pure culture, characterized genomically, then genetically modified before being reintroduced into corals, either by inoculation or injection, including at an early life stage that seems to be a larva or egg bundle.\n\nOverall, the image presents a pipeline from microbiome discovery and culturing to engineered or simplified microbial communities aimed at improving coral health and resilience.",
